11:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L25",null,{"guide":{"date":"June–mid-August 1862, with Kentucky political context from May 1861–April 1862","place":"Northern Mississippi, northern Alabama, Chattanooga and Knoxville in Tennessee, Cumberland Gap, and Kentucky","result":"Braxton Bragg shifted most of the Confederate Army of Mississippi from Tupelo to Chattanooga by a long, indirect rail-and-water route, ending the immediate threat to that city and helping the Confederacy regain the initiative in the western theater. Edmund Kirby Smith then pressed for an independent advance from East Tennessee into Kentucky rather than a preliminary reduction of Cumberland Gap and a prompt junction with Bragg. Bragg accepted a divided offensive despite warning that Don Carlos Buell could defeat the separated armies. Confederate expectations of a mass uprising in Kentucky rested on exaggerated reports and on a provisional government that never displaced the Union-aligned state government","facts":[{"label":"Episode scope","value":"The strategic prelude to the 1862 Confederate invasion of Kentucky","note":"The episode follows the western armies from the aftermath of Corinth through Bragg’s concentration at Chattanooga and the mid-August decision to make Kentucky the principal objective."},{"label":"United States position after Corinth","value":"A broad occupation, not a concentrated pursuit","note":"Henry Halleck dispersed his large force to hold northern Mississippi, western Tennessee, and northern Alabama rather than pursue the Confederate army south from Corinth."},{"label":"Buell’s assignment","value":"Advance toward Chattanooga with roughly 30,000 men","note":"Maj. Gen. Don Carlos Buell moved east along the Memphis and Charleston Railroad, repairing and guarding the line as his army depended on it for supply."},{"label":"Correct name: Ormsby Mitchel","value":"Not “Ormsby Mitchell”","note":"The automated transcript adds a second l. Mitchel’s division already held important portions of the railroad across northern Alabama."},{"label":"Halleck’s new office","value":"General in chief from July 23, 1862","note":"Lincoln summoned Halleck to Washington on July 11. The Army Center of Military History dates his tenure as commanding general from July 23."},{"label":"End of unified western field command","value":"Grant and Buell again operated independently","note":"Halleck’s departure removed the commander who had exercised authority over both armies during the Corinth campaign."},{"label":"Chattanooga’s importance","value":"Tennessee River port, mountain gateway, and railroad junction","note":"Rail connections ran west toward Memphis, northeast through Knoxville toward Virginia, and southeast toward Atlanta. Control threatened Confederate east–west communications and opened routes into East Tennessee and Georgia."},{"label":"Confederate East Tennessee command","value":"Maj. Gen. Edmund Kirby Smith","note":"Smith commanded an independent department and the Army of East Tennessee from Knoxville; he was not automatically Bragg’s subordinate merely because Bragg outranked him."},{"label":"Cumberland Gap occupation","value":"Union forces entered June 18, 1862","note":"Brig. Gen. George W. Morgan occupied the pass after the Confederate garrison withdrew. The position threatened Knoxville but also required supply through difficult mountain country."},{"label":"Buell’s central weakness","value":"A long and repeatedly disrupted railroad supply line","note":"Confederate cavalry and guerrillas damaged track behind the advance, while Buell devoted time and troops to repair and protection."},{"label":"Beauregard’s leave","value":"Departed Tupelo in June without prior approval from Richmond","note":"P. G. T. Beauregard transferred temporary command to Bragg and went to Bladon Springs, Alabama, for his health. Jefferson Davis treated the unauthorized absence as grounds to replace him."},{"label":"Bragg takes command","value":"Army of Mississippi and Department No. 2","note":"Davis made Bragg the permanent western commander in June 1862. The field army was still called the Army of Mississippi during the Kentucky campaign; it became the Army of Tennessee later that year."},{"label":"Forces left in Mississippi","value":"Sterling Price near Tupelo; Earl Van Dorn responsible for Vicksburg","note":"These forces protected northern Mississippi and the remaining Confederate strongpoint on the Mississippi River while Bragg shifted the main field army east."},{"label":"Vicksburg’s position in summer 1862","value":"The principal Confederate barrier remaining on the Mississippi","note":"After the fall of New Orleans, Baton Rouge, Memphis, and other river positions, Union naval forces tried but failed to compel Vicksburg’s surrender."},{"label":"Correct term: Pook Turtles","value":"City-class ironclad gunboats","note":"The transcript renders the nickname as “Pukes.” The vessels designed by Samuel M. Pook were commonly called Pook Turtles."},{"label":"Bragg’s long transfer","value":"About 30,000 men from Tupelo to Chattanooga by rail","note":"Because Buell blocked the direct line, the infantry traveled roughly 770 miles through Mobile, Montgomery, and Atlanta, including a crossing of Mobile Bay. The whole concentration took weeks; individual contingents could complete the journey in about six days."},{"label":"Historical significance of the transfer","value":"First long-distance transfer of an entire field army during the war","note":"The U.S. Army Center of Military History uses this formulation. Cavalry, artillery, wagons, and supplies did not all follow the infantry’s rail route."},{"label":"Chattanooga concentration","value":"First infantry arrived July 29; Bragg arrived July 30","note":"The episode dates the command conference with Kirby Smith to July 31 and the early hours of August 1."},{"label":"Bragg’s initial operational emphasis","value":"Recover Middle Tennessee and threaten Nashville","note":"The developing plan also contemplated a northward movement toward Kentucky and the Ohio River. Surviving interpretations differ on how fully the Kentucky axis was settled at the July 31 conference."},{"label":"Independent command problem","value":"Cooperation depended on consent, not a single theater commander","note":"Bragg commanded Department No. 2 while Smith commanded the separate Department of East Tennessee. Davis corresponded with both, and Bragg lacked normal authority to compel Smith’s compliance."},{"label":"Original Cumberland Gap sequence","value":"Reinforce Smith, reduce the Gap, then join Bragg","note":"Bragg sent two divisions to Smith. Smith argued on August 9 that George Morgan’s well-stocked garrison could withstand a lengthy investment even if its communications were cut."},{"label":"Smith’s alternative","value":"Bypass Cumberland Gap and invade Kentucky","note":"Smith preferred a direct advance north from Knoxville, preserving his independent army and choosing Kentucky rather than Middle Tennessee as his theater of action."},{"label":"Bragg’s warning","value":"Buell could defeat separated Confederate forces","note":"On August 10 Bragg urged concentration against the Army of the Ohio but ultimately accepted Smith’s separate axis and promised cooperation farther north."},{"label":"John Hunt Morgan’s first Kentucky raid","value":"July 1862","note":"The cavalry raid alarmed Union authorities and encouraged Confederate hopes, but Morgan greatly overstated the recruiting response he had found."},{"label":"Morgan’s recruiting prediction","value":"25,000 or 30,000 volunteers","note":"His July 16 dispatch claimed the country could be secured and that tens of thousands would join. The prediction was not borne out during the campaign."},{"label":"Kentucky’s legal status","value":"A United States state that never seceded","note":"Kentucky was a slaveholding border state with divided loyalties. Its elected legislature rejected secession and eventually aligned the state government with the United States."},{"label":"Correct name: Beriah Magoffin","value":"Kentucky’s elected governor","note":"The transcript renders his name phonetically. Magoffin favored neutrality and had Southern sympathies, but he did not have legislative support to take Kentucky out of the Union."},{"label":"Neutrality collapses","value":"September 1861","note":"Confederate Maj. Gen. Leonidas Polk’s occupation of Columbus on September 3 was the first open entry by a belligerent field force. Union troops then occupied Paducah, and the legislature demanded that Confederate forces leave."},{"label":"Russellville Convention","value":"November 18–20, 1861","note":"Pro-Confederate delegates created a provisional government, elected George W. Johnson governor, and challenged the elected government at Frankfort without displacing it."},{"label":"Confederate admission of Kentucky","value":"December 10, 1861","note":"The Confederacy recognized the provisional government and represented Kentucky with a star, although that government exercised power only where Confederate arms permitted."},{"label":"George W. Johnson at Shiloh","value":"Mortally wounded April 7; died April 8, 1862","note":"The provisional governor served with the 4th Kentucky Infantry, was found and aided by Union Brig. Gen. Alexander McDowell McCook, and died aboard a hospital steamer."},{"label":"Provisional successor","value":"Richard Hawes","note":"Hawes became the second Confederate provisional governor and lobbied Richmond for an invasion that could restore the government inside Kentucky."},{"label":"Simultaneous Confederate offensives","value":"Kentucky in the west and northern Virginia/Maryland in the east","note":"Robert E. Lee approved the western movement, but he had to strike John Pope before George B. McClellan could reinforce him. The campaigns were strategically complementary, not synchronized operations."},{"label":"Strategic condition at episode’s end","value":"Confederate initiative restored, but command and supply remained divided","note":"Bragg and Smith entered the campaign with energetic troops and ambitious objectives, yet separate authority, changing plans, and uncertain Kentucky recruitment threatened the undertaking from the outset."},{"label":"Recommended book","value":"“Braxton Bragg: The Most Hated Man of the Confederacy” by Earl J. Hess","note":"The episode recommends Hess’s 2016 biography for a more balanced assessment of Bragg than the general’s hostile historical reputation usually receives."}],"maps":[{"title":"From Tupelo to the Bluegrass State","caption":"This schematic separates two related problems. Bragg first used a long rail-and-water route through Mobile, Montgomery, and Atlanta to concentrate infantry at Chattanooga while Price and Van Dorn remained in Mississippi. Bragg and Kirby Smith then contemplated separate northward axes despite Bragg’s concern that Buell could exploit their division.","source":"Custom diagram based on the U.S. Army Center of Military History campaign history and the Library of Congress 1862 theater map; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep206-kentucky-offensive-plan.svg","alt":"Schematic map of Bragg’s rail transfer from Tupelo through Mobile, Montgomery, and Atlanta to Chattanooga, followed by Bragg’s and Kirby Smith’s separate proposed advances toward Kentucky."},{"title":"A United States Army map of Middle and East Tennessee","caption":"Contextual primary-source image: this 1862 map, prepared for the Army of the Ohio and the Army of the Cumberland, shows rivers, roads, and railroads across the region centered on Nashville and Chattanooga. It supplies period geographic context; it does not plot the episode’s campaign plan.","source":"Geography and Map Division, Library of Congress; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep206-middle-east-tennessee-map.jpg","alt":"An 1862 military map of Middle and East Tennessee and parts of Alabama and Georgia, with railroads marked in red and waterways in blue."},{"title":"Braxton Bragg","caption":"Contextual image: a wartime carte-de-visite portrait of Bragg. In June 1862 he replaced Beauregard in command of the Army of Mississippi and became the principal Confederate field commander shifting forces toward Chattanooga and Kentucky.","source":"Liljenquist Family Collection, Library of Congress; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep206-braxton-bragg.jpg","alt":"Wartime photographic portrait of Confederate General Braxton Bragg in uniform."}],"moments":[{"time":"1:11","audioTime":71,"title":"The western theater story resumes after Corinth","detail":"The episode recalls that Halleck’s capture of the strategic rail junction capped the United States victories from Fort Henry and Donelson through Shiloh."},{"time":"2:33","audioTime":153,"title":"Halleck disperses the victorious army group","detail":"Grant received responsibility around Corinth and Memphis, while Buell took roughly 30,000 men east toward Chattanooga along the Memphis and Charleston Railroad."},{"time":"3:29","audioTime":209,"title":"Halleck is called to Washington","detail":"Lincoln selected him as general in chief in July, ending the period in which Grant and Buell had served under one western field commander."},{"time":"4:20","audioTime":260,"title":"Chattanooga anchors the strategic problem","detail":"The small Tennessee River city stood below Lookout Mountain at the junction of lines leading west, northeast through Knoxville, and southeast toward Atlanta."},{"time":"4:53","audioTime":293,"title":"The railroad hub has consequences far beyond the town","detail":"Its capture would assist East Tennessee Unionists, disrupt Confederate communications between east and west, and open a route toward Georgia."},{"time":"5:14","audioTime":314,"title":"Kirby Smith defends East Tennessee","detail":"From an independent command based at Knoxville, Smith had to consider both Buell’s approach from northern Alabama and George W. Morgan’s presence north of Knoxville."},{"time":"5:38","audioTime":338,"title":"George W. Morgan’s West Point connection is noted","detail":"Morgan and Kirby Smith entered West Point together in 1841; Morgan left the academy before Smith graduated with the class of 1845."},{"time":"5:46","audioTime":346,"title":"United States troops occupy Cumberland Gap","detail":"Morgan entered the pass on June 18 after the Confederate withdrawal, creating a potential approach toward Knoxville but also assuming a difficult supply burden."},{"time":"6:08","audioTime":368,"title":"Buell’s advance loses momentum","detail":"His reliance on a railroad under repeated attack by Confederate cavalry and guerrillas slowed his movement and gave the Confederates time to react."},{"time":"6:50","audioTime":410,"title":"The Confederate field army waits near Tupelo","detail":"The Army of Mississippi had remained south of Corinth after Beauregard’s successful withdrawal at the end of May."},{"time":"6:56","audioTime":416,"title":"Beauregard takes unauthorized medical leave","detail":"He transferred command to Bragg and departed for Bladon Springs without obtaining advance approval from Richmond."},{"time":"7:17","audioTime":437,"title":"Davis makes Bragg the permanent commander","detail":"The leave ended Beauregard’s western command and elevated Bragg, already the army’s senior subordinate, to leadership of the main Confederate field army."},{"time":"7:34","audioTime":454,"title":"Kirby Smith appeals for help","detail":"Richmond had no spare reinforcement for Chattanooga, and Bragg initially appeared tied to northern Mississippi by the United States forces near Corinth."},{"time":"7:59","audioTime":479,"title":"Smith invites Bragg to come east","detail":"He promised cooperation and offered to serve under Bragg if the two independent department commanders brought their armies together."},{"time":"8:34","audioTime":514,"title":"Bragg sees an opportunity to seize the initiative","detail":"The dispersed, inactive United States garrisons in Mississippi made a rapid concentration at Chattanooga appear more useful than remaining at Tupelo."},{"time":"9:17","audioTime":557,"title":"Price and Van Dorn remain behind","detail":"Bragg assigned Sterling Price to watch northern Mississippi and Earl Van Dorn to guard Vicksburg while the main infantry force shifted east."},{"time":"9:45","audioTime":585,"title":"Vicksburg becomes the remaining river barrier","detail":"Farragut’s fleet had taken New Orleans and Baton Rouge, while the federal river flotilla won at Memphis; neither force, however, had reduced Vicksburg."},{"time":"10:18","audioTime":618,"title":"Bragg begins the theater-changing movement","detail":"Cavalry, artillery, wagons, and supplies traveled east across northern Alabama while infantry used a much longer route over the Confederate rail network."},{"time":"10:45","audioTime":645,"title":"The infantry detours through Mobile and Atlanta","detail":"The roughly 770-mile trip avoided the direct railroad controlled by Buell and became the war’s first long-distance transfer of an entire field army."},{"time":"11:08","audioTime":668,"title":"Bragg initially looks toward Middle Tennessee","detail":"Chattanooga could serve as the base for recovering Nashville, forcing Buell onto the defensive, and eventually moving farther north."},{"time":"11:29","audioTime":689,"title":"A western Mississippi diversion is expected","detail":"Bragg anticipated that Price and Van Dorn would threaten Grant, preventing United States reinforcements from reaching Buell."},{"time":"11:49","audioTime":709,"title":"The first infantry reaches Chattanooga","detail":"Some units arrived July 29 and Bragg arrived July 30, but the separate movement of animals, guns, wagons, and supplies delayed full concentration."},{"time":"12:10","audioTime":730,"title":"Bragg and Kirby Smith confer","detail":"Their Chattanooga meeting began July 31 and continued into August 1, producing apparent agreement but not a unified chain of command."},{"time":"12:39","audioTime":759,"title":"Smith hesitates to surrender independence","detail":"Although he spoke cooperatively, his later choices showed reluctance to become Bragg’s subordinate in a campaign centered on Middle Tennessee."},{"time":"13:10","audioTime":790,"title":"Kentucky displaces Nashville in Smith’s planning","detail":"The episode interprets Smith’s conduct after the conference as a decision to preserve his separate army and strike north from Knoxville."},{"time":"13:39","audioTime":819,"title":"Davis expects combined success","detail":"The Confederate president anticipated that action against Buell, reinforced by Price and Van Dorn against Grant, could recover Tennessee and Kentucky."},{"time":"15:59","audioTime":959,"title":"Smith reshapes the plan from Knoxville","detail":"After the advertisement break, the narrative returns to Smith using the delay in Bragg’s concentration to pursue his own operational preference."},{"time":"16:14","audioTime":974,"title":"The original sequence targets Cumberland Gap first","detail":"Bragg would lend two divisions; Smith would reduce Morgan’s garrison and then join Bragg for the movement into Middle Tennessee."},{"time":"16:42","audioTime":1002,"title":"Smith proposes bypassing the Gap","detail":"On August 9 he argued that the well-supplied United States position could resist a long investment even if smaller passes were used to sever its communications."},{"time":"17:39","audioTime":1059,"title":"Bragg warns against divided operations","detail":"His August 10 response recognized Buell as the principal danger and stated that separation could imperil the campaign, yet Bragg did not compel a junction."},{"time":"18:25","audioTime":1105,"title":"Both commanders turn toward Kentucky","detail":"Smith chose the Bluegrass State as his independent field of action, and Bragg accepted a broader northward campaign despite the command risk."},{"time":"18:52","audioTime":1132,"title":"John Hunt Morgan magnifies Confederate hopes","detail":"After his July cavalry raid, Morgan reported that 25,000 or 30,000 Kentuckians would join an invasion—an extravagant prediction the campaign would disprove."},{"time":"19:30","audioTime":1170,"title":"The summer offensive mood spreads","detail":"Western Confederates wanted to reverse the defeats of early 1862, while Lee’s Seven Days victories encouraged expectations of simultaneous gains."},{"time":"20:07","audioTime":1207,"title":"Kentucky’s Confederate delegation presses Davis","detail":"Exiled politicians urged liberation and the use of Kentucky officers for recruiting, projecting tens of thousands of enlistments."},{"time":"20:39","audioTime":1239,"title":"A Confederate star did not equal legal secession","detail":"Kentucky never left the United States; Confederate recognition rested on a provisional government created by a pro-secession minority."},{"time":"21:07","audioTime":1267,"title":"Kentucky’s neutrality breaks down","detail":"Polk’s occupation of Columbus in September 1861 was followed by United States entry at Paducah and a legislative demand that Confederate forces withdraw."},{"time":"21:24","audioTime":1284,"title":"The Russellville Convention creates a rival government","detail":"Pro-Confederate delegates repudiated the elected government at Frankfort and chose George W. Johnson as provisional governor."},{"time":"21:53","audioTime":1313,"title":"The provisional government follows the army into exile","detail":"Recognized by the Confederacy on December 10, 1861, it retreated from Bowling Green after Forts Henry and Donelson fell."},{"time":"22:14","audioTime":1334,"title":"George W. Johnson is mortally wounded at Shiloh","detail":"Serving with the 4th Kentucky Infantry, he was struck on April 7, received aid after capture, and died the next day."},{"time":"22:35","audioTime":1355,"title":"Richard Hawes succeeds Johnson","detail":"The second provisional governor remained a refugee and advocated a Confederate campaign that could return his government to Kentucky."},{"time":"22:47","audioTime":1367,"title":"Davis seeks theater-wide coordination","detail":"As commander in chief, he tried to align Bragg’s and Smith’s western movements with Lee’s offensive against United States forces in Virginia."},{"time":"22:59","audioTime":1379,"title":"Lee approves but cannot wait","detail":"Lee supported the proposed Kentucky movement, yet the need to strike John Pope before McClellan could reinforce him prevented close synchronization."},{"time":"23:40","audioTime":1420,"title":"The western strategic picture reverses","detail":"By mid-August the Confederates had regained initiative, but their Kentucky campaign began with divided authority, changing objectives, and persistent supply risks."},{"time":"24:29","audioTime":1469,"title":"The episode recommends Earl J. Hess’s Bragg biography","detail":"“Braxton Bragg: The Most Hated Man of the Confederacy” is presented as a careful reassessment of a commander long treated with unusual hostility."}],"command":[{"side":"United States national and western command","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"President Abraham Lincoln","command":"Commander in chief","subordinates":"Made East Tennessee a longstanding strategic objective and appointed Halleck general in chief"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Henry W. Halleck","command":"General in chief from July 23, 1862","subordinates":"Left western field command for Washington; Grant and Buell then resumed independent army commands"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Don Carlos Buell","command":"Army of the Ohio","subordinates":"Advanced toward Chattanooga; Brig. Gen. George W. Morgan commanded the division at Cumberland Gap, while Ormsby Mitchel’s division held parts of northern Alabama"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Ulysses S. Grant","command":"Army of the Tennessee and District of West Tennessee","subordinates":"Held the Corinth–Memphis sector independently of Buell and was the intended target of Price and Van Dorn’s diversion"}]},{"side":"Confederate national and main western command","color":"#963b31","branches":[{"commander":"President Jefferson Davis","command":"Commander in chief","subordinates":"Replaced Beauregard with Bragg and attempted to coordinate Bragg, Kirby Smith, and Lee without creating a unified western command"},{"commander":"Gen. Braxton Bragg","command":"Department No. 2 and Army of Mississippi","subordinates":"Moved the main field army to Chattanooga; assigned Sterling Price near Tupelo and Earl Van Dorn to Vicksburg; lent two divisions to Kirby Smith"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Sterling Price","command":"Confederate force near Tupelo","subordinates":"Watched the United States forces in northern Mississippi and was expected eventually to cooperate against Grant"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Earl Van Dorn","command":"District of the Mississippi","subordinates":"Held responsibility for defending Vicksburg and was expected to cooperate with Price against Grant"}]},{"side":"Confederate East Tennessee command","color":"#a45a36","branches":[{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Edmund Kirby Smith","command":"Department and Army of East Tennessee","subordinates":"Defended Knoxville and Chattanooga, received two divisions from Bragg, and chose a separate advance into Kentucky"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Carter L. Stevenson","command":"Division in East Tennessee","subordinates":"Had withdrawn from Cumberland Gap in June and remained one of Smith’s senior field commanders"},{"commander":"Col. John Hunt Morgan","command":"Cavalry raiding force","subordinates":"Raided Kentucky in July and sent Smith an exaggerated estimate of the recruits an invasion would produce"}]},{"side":"Kentucky civil authority and rival claimant","color":"#75634b","branches":[{"commander":"Gov. Beriah Magoffin","command":"Elected governor of Kentucky","subordinates":"Favored neutrality and held Southern sympathies but governed with a Unionist legislature that rejected secession"},{"commander":"Kentucky General Assembly","command":"Lawful state legislature","subordinates":"Aligned the state with the United States after Confederate troops occupied Columbus in September 1861"},{"commander":"George W. Johnson; then Richard Hawes","command":"Confederate provisional governors","subordinates":"Led the rival government created at Russellville, recognized by Richmond but unable to displace the government at Frankfort"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Henry W. Halleck","role":"Former western department commander summoned to Washington and installed as United States general in chief","side":"United States","source":"https://history.army.mil/Research/Reference-Topics/-CONTINENTAL-ARMY-GENERAL-AND-COMMANDER-IN-CHIEF/"},{"personId":"ulysses-s-grant","role":"Army of the Tennessee commander left to hold the Corinth–Memphis region after Halleck’s departure"},{"name":"Don Carlos Buell","role":"Army of the Ohio commander whose slow, supply-bound advance toward Chattanooga gave the Confederates time to concentrate","side":"United States","source":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-7.pdf"},{"name":"Ormsby M. Mitchel","role":"United States division commander holding parts of northern Alabama along Buell’s line of advance","side":"United States","source":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-7.pdf"},{"name":"George W. Morgan","role":"United States division commander who occupied Cumberland Gap and threatened Knoxville from the north","side":"United States","source":"https://www.nps.gov/cuga/civil-war-at-cumberland-gap.htm"},{"name":"David G. Farragut","role":"Flag officer whose Gulf squadron captured New Orleans and Baton Rouge and then operated against Vicksburg","side":"United States","source":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/david-farragut.htm"},{"name":"Alexander McDowell McCook","role":"United States brigadier general and prewar acquaintance who arranged care for the mortally wounded George W. Johnson after Shiloh","side":"United States","source":"https://apps.legislature.ky.gov/LegislativeMoments/moments09RS/web/Lincoln%20moments%2017.pdf"},{"name":"P. G. T. Beauregard","role":"Army of Mississippi commander replaced after leaving for Bladon Springs without prior approval from Richmond","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://www.nps.gov/people/pgt-beauregard.htm"},{"name":"Braxton Bragg","role":"Army of Mississippi commander who shifted the field army to Chattanooga and accepted a divided offensive into Kentucky","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/ep206-braxton-bragg.jpg","source":"https://www.nps.gov/chch/learn/historyculture/braxton-bragg.htm"},{"name":"Edmund Kirby Smith","role":"Independent East Tennessee commander who pressed to bypass Cumberland Gap and advance directly into Kentucky","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/ep206-kirby-smith.jpg","source":"https://www.nps.gov/vick/learn/historyculture/edmund-kirby-smith.htm"},{"name":"Sterling Price","role":"Confederate major general left near Tupelo to watch United States forces in northern Mississippi","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-7.pdf"},{"name":"Earl Van Dorn","role":"Confederate major general assigned responsibility for Vicksburg and later cooperation against Grant","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-7.pdf"},{"name":"John Hunt Morgan","role":"Kentucky-born Confederate cavalry commander whose July raid and recruiting claims encouraged the invasion plan","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/today-in-history/october-08/"},{"personId":"nathan-bedford-forrest","role":"Confederate cavalry commander whose raids contributed to the insecurity of Buell’s railroad communications"},{"name":"Jefferson Davis","role":"Confederate president who replaced Beauregard, encouraged offensive action, and tried to coordinate separate commanders","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-7.pdf"},{"name":"George W. Johnson","role":"First Confederate provisional governor of Kentucky, mortally wounded while serving with the 4th Kentucky at Shiloh","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://apps.legislature.ky.gov/LegislativeMoments/moments09RS/web/Lincoln%20moments%2017.pdf"},{"name":"Richard Hawes","role":"Johnson’s successor as Confederate provisional governor and an advocate for the invasion of Kentucky","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://history.ky.gov/news/civil-war-governors-of-kentucky-digital-documentary-launches-new-website"},{"name":"John C. Breckinridge","role":"Kentucky-born Confederate general with whom George W. Johnson served as a volunteer aide before joining the ranks at Shiloh","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://apps.legislature.ky.gov/LegislativeMoments/moments09RS/web/Lincoln%20moments%2017.pdf"},{"name":"Robert E. Lee","role":"Army of Northern Virginia commander who approved the western offensive but moved against John Pope on an independent timetable","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-7.pdf"}],"losses":[{"name":"George W. Johnson","side":"Confederacy","role":"Confederate provisional governor of Kentucky serving with the 4th Kentucky Infantry at Shiloh","outcome":"Mortally wounded on April 7, 1862, the second day of Shiloh; captured and treated by United States personnel; died April 8 aboard the hospital steamer Hannibal."}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/ep206-kentucky-offensive-plan.svg","alt":"Schematic of Bragg’s rail transfer to Chattanooga and the separate Confederate axes proposed toward Middle Tennessee and Kentucky.","caption":"The operational problem in one view: an innovative concentration shifted the initiative, but no comparable consolidation of command followed it.","source":"Custom diagram based on U.S. Army Center of Military History and Library of Congress sources."},{"image":"/images/ep206-middle-east-tennessee-map.jpg","alt":"An 1862 military map covering Middle and East Tennessee and adjacent parts of Alabama and Georgia.","caption":"Contextual primary-source image: this Army of the Ohio and Army of the Cumberland map marks railroads in red and waterways in blue. It gives period geographic context but does not itself depict Bragg’s 1862 movement.","source":"Geography and Map Division, Library of Congress."},{"image":"/images/ep206-braxton-bragg.jpg","alt":"Wartime photographic portrait of Confederate General Braxton Bragg in uniform.","caption":"Contextual image: a wartime portrait of Bragg, who replaced Beauregard and moved the Army of Mississippi to Chattanooga before turning toward Kentucky.","source":"Liljenquist Family Collection, Library of Congress."},{"image":"/images/ep206-kirby-smith.jpg","alt":"Wartime glass-negative portrait of Confederate General Edmund Kirby Smith in uniform.","caption":"Contextual image: a wartime portrait of Kirby Smith, whose independent departmental authority and preference for a direct Kentucky advance shaped the campaign before it began.","source":"Civil War Glass Negatives and Related Prints, Library of Congress."}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Civil War Podcast page containing episode 206","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/category/1862/page/5/"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: The Civil War in the Western Theater, 1862","url":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-7.pdf"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: commanding generals and Halleck’s tenure","url":"https://history.army.mil/Research/Reference-Topics/-CONTINENTAL-ARMY-GENERAL-AND-COMMANDER-IN-CHIEF/"},{"label":"U.S. Army staff ride handbook for the Perryville Campaign","url":"https://www.armyupress.army.mil/Portals/7/educational-services/staff-rides/StaffRideHB_Perryville.pdf"},{"label":"National Park Service: Chattanooga, Tennessee—Train Town","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/chattanooga-tennessee-train-town-teaching-with-historic-places.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Civil War at Cumberland Gap","url":"https://www.nps.gov/cuga/civil-war-at-cumberland-gap.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Braxton Bragg biography","url":"https://www.nps.gov/chch/learn/historyculture/braxton-bragg.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Edmund Kirby Smith biography","url":"https://www.nps.gov/vick/learn/historyculture/edmund-kirby-smith.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: David Farragut biography","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/david-farragut.htm"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Fighting for Kentucky","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/today-in-history/october-08/"},{"label":"Kentucky Historical Society: Kentucky History at a Glance","url":"https://history.ky.gov/kentucky-history-at-a-glance"},{"label":"Kentucky Historical Society: proceedings of the Russellville Convention","url":"https://history.ky.gov/news/introducing-the-proceedings-of-the-shadowy-government-of-kentucky"},{"label":"Kentucky General Assembly: Confederate Governor George W. Johnson","url":"https://apps.legislature.ky.gov/LegislativeMoments/moments09RS/web/Lincoln%20moments%2017.pdf"},{"label":"Primary source: Beauregard’s June 15, 1862, leave letter and related correspondence","url":"https://archive.org/details/53953815.4122.emory.edu"},{"label":"Library of Congress: 1862 map of Middle and East Tennessee","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2006636344/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: wartime portrait of Braxton Bragg","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2022630536/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: wartime portrait of Edmund Kirby Smith","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666549/"},{"label":"Official podcast book-recommendations list","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/book-recommendations/"}]}}]}]
12:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section maps-section","id":"maps","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Maps"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"maps-grid","children":[["$","figure","A United States Army map of Middle and East Tennessee",{"className":"reference-map","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"map-frame static-map-frame","children":["$","a",null,{"className":"map-image-link","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep206-middle-east-tennessee-map.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","aria-label":"Open A United States Army map of Middle and East Tennessee at full size","children":["$","img",null,{"src":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep206-middle-east-tennessee-map.jpg","alt":"An 1862 military map of Middle and East Tennessee and parts of Alabama and Georgia, with railroads marked in red and waterways in blue.","width":1600,"height":1200,"loading":"lazy"}]}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"A United States Army map of Middle and East Tennessee"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Contextual primary-source image: this 1862 map, prepared for the Army of the Ohio and the Army of the Cumberland, shows rivers, roads, and railroads across the region centered on Nashville and Chattanooga. It supplies period geographic context; it does not plot the episode’s campaign plan."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"map-actions","children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep206-middle-east-tennessee-map.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","children":["Open full-size map ",["$","$Le",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$d:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:children:2:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-up-right","size":13}]]}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Geography and Map Division, Library of Congress; see sources below."}]]}],"$undefined"]}]]}],["$","figure","Braxton Bragg",{"className":"reference-map","children":[["$","div",nu